  "structure": {
    "block_count": 31,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "100 Years of Car Seat History in (About) 60 Seconds",
        "text_sha256": "d4a66de0ad012426a5a6f2f738f69661e3594a303636bd26c42964d8f2953c92",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Early car seats were designed to help a child see outside the car. Safety wasn’t a priority.",
        "text_sha256": "841f5fae595e65bf155a4ff771c7ac777416d7683fe1cf3ee9865bdc7d934bbc",
        "word_count": 17
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1933 - Bunny Bear Company produces a booster seat so parents can keep an eye on kids in the car.",
        "text_sha256": "d0e991ba38295f6995f35d0f236e0033f4e5ccfed55c0c10049b2711469074d1",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1940s - Canvas seats on a metal frame attach to a car’s front seat so kids can get a better view through the windshield.",
        "text_sha256": "371506e178b5919d09f1907389a68858b031628ac2fafd52ce62b0595122f1c0",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1962 - Finally, the first car seats designed for safety is released. In England, Jean Ames created a rear-facing seat with a safety harness similar to today’s models.Meanwhile, in America Len Rivkin designed a metal-framed seat with a safety buckle.",
        "text_sha256": "1f5421700d891fe0294d1a52c151b42cfa6cf65a604661f17e5fbd3c06d1d7ae",
        "word_count": 40
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1968 - Auto manufacturers get into the game with the first child car seats designed for crash protection. Ford develops the Tot-Guard. GM develops the Love Seat.",
        "text_sha256": "456bb93795d78b1308e9d59000cd327e02ff2f52997837d3c3aed7461baba341",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1970s - The Bobby Mac convertible seat is a hit with parents. The Bobby Mac car seat could be use dread-facing or forward-facing.",
        "text_sha256": "e53abc8190be0df15572715d6df86cba4409f836082babfd74e0d07378798b8a",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1971 - The first federal standards for car seat safety require a three-point child harness as well as a safety belt to attach the car seat to the vehicle.",
        "text_sha256": "bdf5e191a9a15d526267c875ac02fb4dfb742d2fe1a61b1bd588209012cab8b4",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1980s - Crash testing for car seats is required. Car seats gradually become sturdier and more protective.",
        "text_sha256": "30bdce18dd65e57686691099ffe6694f9968d82767d2d3d5906e826dcea6afb9",
        "word_count": 17
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "1997 - ISofix, a precursor to today’s LATCH (Lower, Anchors & Tethers for Children) system, is the first attempt at an anchor system for securing child car seats.",
        "text_sha256": "598ed7bfc1ff80d18398bb390cda2cedc2ba773838c5c3c5f558e73aa9b43e2c",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "2002 - LATCH is mandated by law . Washington and California pass the first booster seat laws for children over 40 pounds.",
        "text_sha256": "278b311d7f7cf36247b47801abe48ffe974eebdc8e60b19a6f89bbe490af208c",
        "word_count": 22
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "2000s - Car seat technology takes a huge leap forward. Infant car seats, 3-in-1 car seats, and travel system car seats all debut in the U.S.",
        "text_sha256": "77cf88ee5a0d8fd47a00037f3ea548fc6f2912fcea708048ae1cbd152ccd30bb",
        "word_count": 26
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Today - All car seats must meet safety standards but some models offer additional feature not currently required by law like Kim Kardashian’s Orbit Baby G2 (retail price $1200).",
        "text_sha256": "88652dd19375771883078b54cf8ef59a43fb4992048a6fca58fbfd25859de3ee",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your Next Read",
        "text_sha256": "6d2b65f6bc74d3f3d5656d8c88c806ff70605a0ce9d4fd990efe9af093220755",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
